update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('6ced6357-3ec5-411d-b528-71cfd29da3ae'::uuid,'872b493bb2cdd2465489030bf8e0ddd6','The stimulus never compares the purchasing power of highly trained workers with that of less-trained workers. The lower prices helped "workers" in general. Training level shows up only in the claim about job security, so A takes a variable from one premise and attaches it to a different outcome.'),
('9d8e7cd8-046a-44e4-9480-6bcc33cdce85'::uuid,'73ca18e2d8258d5dd5c63babd5744ae8','Two problems. The stimulus says workers gained something: they could buy goods they could not afford before, so "decreased benefits" ignores the one benefit we''re told about. And profits for owners never appear; the stimulus says owners could lower prices, not that they earned more. B needs facts the premises don''t give.'),
('154ab80a-e668-47f5-91bf-97f8ec1f16c7'::uuid,'2745ea0f9e2ae1866ac8585aab09ed7f','The only comparison about job security says highly trained workers are less vulnerable than workers with little training. That points toward better security for the trained, not toward "never" having security. The stimulus says nothing about "the highest paid employees" at all, and "never" is far stronger than a comparison can support.'),
('9f890e38-0bef-4493-8ae7-62f06ab0c2f3'::uuid,'2016ef113a267375d6bad11a588156f9','Match it to the prediction piece by piece. "a source of increased purchasing power" is mass production and the lower prices it brought. "also undermines their job security" is the pivot: those same techniques rely on workers with little training, and those jobs are more vulnerable to elimination. D keeps both halves the stimulus set up, and it stays modest. The word "undermines" only says security is weakened, which is what "more vulnerable to elimination" gives us. On a completion that follows a "But", look for the answer that holds the gain and the cost together instead of picking one side.'),
('2946bed1-b948-4443-811c-2afcc367992f'::uuid,'6f9f3fc5cfbf0276679c6644c82a4266','This is the tempting overreach. "more vulnerable to elimination" describes a risk, not jobs that have actually disappeared. To say the share of workers who can afford these goods is "shrinking", we''d need real job losses, and we''d need them to outweigh the lower prices. Test it: could every worker still have a job and still afford the goods? Yes, so E isn''t supported.'),
('7e2a9b81-ee94-424d-a5c3-f552c52c4924'::uuid,'e6d56e6c101407f319bafce27787bd87','This tempts because it also turns the yield story into money. But "uneconomic" means the pesticides cost more than they return. We only know yield slides down from an elevated level; it could stay above the no-pesticide yield forever, and we have no figures on what the pesticides cost. A shrinking benefit is still a benefit, so A goes further than the evidence.'),
('83a3ece8-e662-4e6d-a2f2-850fb5c50ca9'::uuid,'c98a03e153d0d9e78bd258b17ac56e0d','The stimulus is only about crop yield. Environmental damage is a new topic, and nothing in the premises tells us anything about it. Pesticides may well harm the environment in real life, but a completion has to follow from what the stimulus says, not from what we know elsewhere.'),
('bd6d008c-6e20-4c93-96a2-1e91ea55b8e1'::uuid,'177307430b2a654c605959ca57515106','Pests never come up; the evidence is about yield. And "unmanageable" is extreme. A gradual decline in yield from a high point fits perfectly well with pests being fully under control, so C adds both a new topic and a much stronger claim than the evidence allows.'),
('9ed37f3e-07e3-4bc4-8df4-06738ef239c2'::uuid,'5a0e4cdd97a85fb8a895a7af390ff7cf','The stimulus says nothing about what the manufacturers recommend, so we can''t judge whether farmers follow it. Yields could decline even when the pesticides are used exactly as directed. "probably" softens the claim, but softening an unsupported idea doesn''t make it supported.'),
('022f6e42-13ae-4db2-8c25-d2fa9a1a0988'::uuid,'807df4bb489f02ad62608d8bf67db0b4','Line it up with the evidence. The "financial returns" are the yield gains: a bigger crop is worth more, so the step from yield to money is a small one. "diminish over time" is the gradual slide from the initially elevated level. E doesn''t say the returns vanish or turn into losses; it only says they get smaller, which is exactly what "depresses crop yield from this initially elevated level" allows. When a completion follows a concession, look for the answer that tempers the good news without overturning it.'),
('c56c4f1f-6939-4942-98ad-6e87e08c6a46'::uuid,'0077fd5f10b75aa4ff768516376a0644','The puzzle is that more fast MMs reach the detector than the lab decay rate allows. If the trip took longer than supposed, the MMs would have more time to decay, so we''d expect even fewer to arrive. A points the wrong way, and nothing in the stimulus questions the travel time anyway.'),
('dad6ec9a-c721-42e9-bb65-79d926fa7d73'::uuid,'461b8ece5136a8e69d004dfc4b52ff13','If the equipment struggled to detect MMs, we''d undercount them, which makes the high count we actually get even harder to explain. The conditional connects decay rate to the number detected. How hard detection is says nothing about the decay rate the blank has to address.'),
('7c681cb0-4e99-4558-834a-15c37ad3e737'::uuid,'7241f53d6d52e07432f2341710a8143e','This is the most tempting wrong turn, because the stimulus is full of counts. But fewer MMs to begin with would mean fewer detected, and the problem is that we detect far more than the lab decay rate predicts. A smaller supply makes that mismatch worse. The only way out the conditional leaves open is about how fast the MMs decay, and C says nothing about decay.'),
('a48a5fd3-abfd-424c-8a81-3fbc58d28d53'::uuid,'532e7291d5851d7148d5d89b8a652a1f','This is the contrapositive put into words. The conditional said: if fast MMs decay as fast as lab MMs, we''d detect only one one-hundredth of what we actually detect. We obviously detect what we detect, so the "if" part must be false: fast MMs do not decay as fast as lab MMs. "decay more slowly than mu mesons almost at rest" says exactly that, and "almost at rest" describes the lab MMs. When a stimulus hands you a conditional whose result can''t be true, deny its trigger and look for the answer that says so.'),
('6b4dbfd4-1696-4a2b-9b03-ac0d4cdb8213'::uuid,'f9ba59a78dd3b098a6abebaee67e6f48','The first sentence says these MMs are "generated by cosmic rays", and nothing later casts doubt on that. E contradicts a premise instead of completing the argument. Where the MMs come from also has nothing to do with how fast they decay, which is the only thing the conditional lets us conclude about.'),
('175a8805-a733-4fa6-bed5-5f25661c481e'::uuid,'c89419de42282935e276e8f5d45abc20','The stimulus says the theory "has had no success" explaining gravity''s strength. That reports the outcome, not the effort. The theorists may have tried hard and failed. A assumes they haven''t tried, and it also predicts that trying would help, which nothing in the stimulus supports.'),
('43a540f3-4cc3-44ed-8310-01c388dfdc62'::uuid,'dbc818c1c7c593be30240e7a6d66c7e6','The stimulus compares superstring theory with established theories on one point only: superstring theory purports to explain gravity''s nature and existence, and they don''t. It never says they explain physical phenomena better. They might explain less, since they don''t even attempt that. B builds a general ranking out of a single difference.'),
('540c51d2-ba18-45b2-a306-fc3e72ea3496'::uuid,'3c23a1121f1166127caf1645c6a4a526','This is the trap that sounds careful, because "some" and "at least some success" are weak wording. But the stimulus tells us established theories don''t even purport to explain the nature and existence of gravity, and it says nothing about any of them explaining gravity''s strength. "at least some success" is still a claim of success, and we have no evidence for it. Weak wording doesn''t rescue a claim with no support.'),
('80587f22-545d-49c1-97d3-f1fc07fc947d'::uuid,'1c18e09c5768a1ebc3a5e814f4d365da','The giant accelerator is a "problem" for testing the theory. A problem for testing is not proof that the theory is false. A theory could be true even if we can never build the machine to check it. "cannot be true" goes far past a stimulus that only says testing is impractical.'),
('b2b13079-613b-4567-8c71-6675493b3b04'::uuid,'92b2496c26624c32f9cba02a98016f1d','Start with the word "problem". The stimulus treats the failure to explain why gravity is "not stronger or weaker than it is" as a problem for a theory that claims to explain the nature and existence of gravity. E states the standard that makes that complaint sensible: a theory that purports to explain the nature of a force is deficient if it cannot account for the force''s strength. "deficient" matches "problem" in strength; it doesn''t say the theory is false or useless. When a stimulus labels something a problem or a weakness, ask what standard the label assumes. An answer stating that standard is often the best supported.'),
('dd98680f-34ea-443d-87b1-edd626d2abbe'::uuid,'1009ae9005e24b77dfed98ed2cbbd07c','This is the economists'' second claim, the one the "But" sets up to attack. Cerrato''s premises point the other way: companies are unlikely to replace satisfied employees with dissatisfied ones, so dissatisfied part-timers are unlikely to take full-timers'' jobs. A completes the argument with the opposing position.'),
('c4187029-612b-49dc-823f-b5ccba691768'::uuid,'a35b19ab7bc09bfab0d9d8416cba8915','Profits never come up, and pay appears only in the phrase "lower-paid". Cerrato''s premises connect satisfaction, efficiency, and who replaces whom. Nothing there tells us which companies earn the most. B needs a premise linking high wages to high profits, and the stimulus never gives one.'),
('1df36749-55c2-44e2-85c5-e7b207125490'::uuid,'1199037d971931e8b2b32288126839df','Follow the chain. The economists say part-timers are the dissatisfied group. Cerrato adds that companies are unlikely to replace satisfied employees with dissatisfied ones. So dissatisfied part-timers are unlikely to replace the more satisfied full-timers. C says exactly that, and its wording is careful: by saying "dissatisfied part-time workers", C doesn''t need part-timers to really be dissatisfied. It only says that if they are, the threat claim fails. When a completion answers someone else''s argument, look for the answer that uses one of their own claims against the other.'),
('d2a63931-4bb0-4445-a711-48f357a39824'::uuid,'57bb3caeb43a61770d1d3fbf66c18d8a','Cerrato never asks what causes the higher turnover. The economists use turnover as evidence of dissatisfaction, and Cerrato starts from the dissatisfaction. Whether it explains all or only part of the turnover has no bearing on whether companies would replace full-timers with part-timers, so D doesn''t finish the reasoning the "But" began.'),
('3eb79107-60fa-4b94-898f-5d89f428ca0f'::uuid,'90f02f99f41ac1dbe36f61030df0d69b','Nothing in the stimulus says when companies choose to hire part-timers. E adds a hiring policy out of nowhere. It also misses the target: the blank should say whether part-timers threaten full-time jobs, and E answers a different question about the conditions for hiring them.'),
('ec2a420e-b8ce-4005-8f12-32cef9951a18'::uuid,'9dc656a6558fd509dea5dc48c04275f3','This is the chain we built. Statement 3 puts certain sage-nectar strains among the most healthful, and statement 2 says all of the most healthful strains are unusually high in antioxidants. So some sage-nectar strains are unusually high in antioxidants. "Some" is all we can claim, since we only know about "certain strains". A leaves out "primarily", but honey from bees harvesting primarily sage nectar is still honey from bees harvesting sage nectar, so A still follows. When a rule says all of a group has a trait and a fact puts something in that group, the trait carries over.'),
('98a2ad07-a76a-403c-b935-741a1b698946'::uuid,'9fd01e4a1f28d5d6608a577df00318b2','The stimulus mentions one nectar source, sage, and says nothing about what most plants produce. One light-colored example can''t tell us about the majority of plants. B needs a count the stimulus never gives.'),
('03c90b3e-528c-4f3b-aec7-82c13d910107'::uuid,'0c6a8ba1ac76fffaafcdc4e3abf6e23b','The only general trend in the stimulus is about antioxidants, and it runs toward dark honey. The light sage strains arrive with "However", as an exception, and a few light strains among the most healthful can''t tell us how light honey does as a whole. The stimulus never compares light and dark honey on healthfulness, so C invents a tendency.'),
('ac3092ed-6094-460d-8c50-0d1500af91b5'::uuid,'6b587e7cf34d124954db3d625a8c93c4','This is the light-color trap. If you apply statement 1''s trend to these light strains, you''d expect low antioxidants. But the only sage strains we know about are the ones among the most healthful, and those must be unusually high in antioxidants because all of the most healthful strains are. Could some other sage strain be low? Maybe, but the stimulus gives no evidence of one, so D has no support.'),
('724397e8-0b37-4d4d-a097-7f7f30a2f2bf'::uuid,'02f1d164c5074165c7c8bfbd6726a6aa','We know some light strains are unusually high in antioxidants. That doesn''t make one of them the highest. "among the most healthful" isn''t the single most healthful, and "unusually high" isn''t highest. Since dark honey tends to have more antioxidants, the top honey could easily be dark. E needs a ranking the stimulus never gives.'),
('8f511f5e-911f-4c6d-88c5-2e4418e581d2'::uuid,'19d8f9f276e9f25c78d1eeb7fb6964ae','The sociologist''s premise covers every culture: "each of its traditions is essential" to its identity. A culture''s ability to reflect on its customs doesn''t change that. A says the strategy can save some cultures, which sides with the anthropologists the "But" is arguing against.'),
('bfd05df2-16ad-4a38-a16e-148fdbc232d7'::uuid,'3de58cd6bddfc1c390de2002ce091942','Chain it. The strategy gives up many traditions. Each tradition is essential to the culture''s identity. A culture that loses what is essential to its identity no longer has that identity, so the strategy doesn''t preserve the culture; it ends it. That''s why B can say "ensure the elimination" instead of something milder: "essential" is a strong word, and B uses its full force. "rather than prevent its decay" points back to the anthropologists'' goal and says the strategy produces the opposite. When a premise calls something essential, ask what happens when it''s removed. Often the answer is that the thing itself is gone.'),
('7d4f0d8f-fd00-4db7-89c4-17d161b3681b'::uuid,'756e7e4b2833d1d31a82393a3b40c9e3','Which cultures anthropologists study has nothing to do with whether giving up essential traditions destroys a culture. "all and only" also makes a sweeping claim about who can carry out the strategy, and the sociologist says nothing about that. C completes a different argument.'),
('ea2d9fc3-f9ba-4f04-be79-6947532dfa28'::uuid,'50867c1ccd1ddaa466cf0be48c7d912e','This is the anthropologists'' claim, nearly word for word. "But since" signals that the sociologist is about to reject it, so the blank needs the sociologist''s objection, not a restatement of the view under attack.'),
('9df27a6f-3c51-4329-af28-bc801041a41d'::uuid,'5d357191484a7247b797a59a7fb9ce02','The premise says "each" tradition is essential, old or new. How recently a tradition was adopted doesn''t change that, so losing many recent traditions still costs the culture its identity. E invents an exception the premise rules out.'),
('f2093996-b3cf-4ee8-a342-b41e2cf75833'::uuid,'748cfeaf226fccead7ad28d88c3d26df','This matches the prediction. Infants on boiled cow''s milk got scurvy, a vitamin C deficiency, and breast-fed infants didn''t. The comparison that explains that split is A: boiled cow''s milk makes less vitamin C available than the same amount of mother''s milk. "the same amount" keeps the comparison fair, so it doesn''t depend on how much milk anyone drank. The passage doesn''t prove A, but A is the explanation the facts point to. When one difference between groups lines up with a difference in outcome, the best-supported answer usually links the two.'),
('2a142f2c-1ddd-49a2-8588-7b0389f2655f'::uuid,'5975e3f418abeea1ce5c3f88b3c8208e','B''s "potentially fatal" matches the passage, so that part is fine. The problem is "frequently". Doctors can advise against a risk without it being common, and the passage never says how often unboiled milk made infants sick. B adds a rate the passage doesn''t give.'),
('70063f86-d63a-4ef7-bc2e-a068ffcc7d5b'::uuid,'296f7e2b3e48571e0943466d6d32af5c','Nothing connects mother''s milk to gastrointestinal infections. The infection risk is raised only for unboiled cow''s milk, and all we learn about breast-fed infants is that they didn''t get scurvy. C attaches the risk to the wrong milk.'),
('ba3e0fe2-b08c-42db-add7-55d3f35b3946'::uuid,'3976a49ac39490509df109893d13bdcb','The passage tells us scurvy is caused by vitamin C deficiency, but not what the doctors knew when they gave the advice. They may have known the cause and simply not realized boiling affected vitamin C. D is a claim about the doctors'' knowledge, and the passage gives no evidence either way.'),
('fef97640-a56c-45b4-9f03-26afd4f00a30'::uuid,'cbb1467f55610ea2b4926dcfc9a249b4','The passage says infants who weren''t breast-fed got cow''s milk, but never how many there were. "most mothers" requires a proportion, and an alarming rise in scurvy could come from a minority of infants. Nothing supports E''s count.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;